<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zaydism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zaydi Shia Islam, Zaydi Shi'ism, Zaydiyya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zaydism is a branch of Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> named after Zayd ibn Ali, a great-grandson of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Ali, who led a revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> in 740. Zaydis accept as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> any qualified descendant of Hasan or Husayn who claims the office and takes up arms, and they stand close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> in law. Zaydi states arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> in the 9th century and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, where Zaydi imams ruled until 1962.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zaydism.jpg" alt="Zaydism" width="320" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M.Schoettler · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yemen-2005-IMG_20181129_0024b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
